sjdevnull@yahoo.com wrote:

> I think you're missing something: you can't take actions just to
> minimize the odds of what actually winds up killing you.  Without
> hindsight, you must at each step take the action that has the highest
> odds of survival in the face of all possible threats.  Sometimes that
> will _not_ be the optimal action for what winds up killing you, but
> it's still a better play overall (in terms of actually maximizing your
> ascension percentage).
 
> That means there's some cutoff point where you stop searching and
> move.  And that means there's a chance of a death that is not
> avoidable through optimal play (even if that death may, in fact, be
> avoidable through sub-par play that increases your overall odds of
> dying).

Mathematically this is sorta related to the Kelly criterion 
(http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kelly_criterion) for making an 
optimal bet.  

Every turn, you need to pick the action with the highest marginal
return of survival odds.  Searching for the Nth time reduces the 
risk of death by (1/8)^N * (trapdamage/hitpoints) or some formula 
like that (actually a lot more complicated since traps do a lot 
of different things besides just affecting hitpoints). NOT searching 
for the Nth time reduces the risk of death by something hard-to-
calculate relating to your current food supply, availability of 
predictable other food, your hunger state, how much risk there 
is in forgoing for another turn possession of whatever's behind 
the door or in the box, etc.

At the point where these two functions of N have the same marginal 
return in terms of reduced risk, the optimal strategy changes from 
searching to not searching.

Sometimes the optimal bet loses, but that never changes the fact 
that it was the optimal bet. 

